<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    Splitlane macOS .app Info.plist template.

    scripts/bundle-macos.sh replaces @VERSION@ with the value of its
    version flag at bundle-assembly time (e.g. "0.2.0"), populating both
    CFBundleVersion (build number) and CFBundleShortVersionString
    (user-visible release). This keeps the two fields in lock-step: we
    ship one version number per release and never increment a separate
    build counter.

    CFBundleIconFile=Splitlane matches the filename Splitlane.icns that
    the bundle script drops into Contents/Resources/ (macOS resolves the
    filename without the .icns extension).

    LSMinimumSystemVersion=13.0 (Ventura) is the floor because GPUI's
    macOS backend targets that era. Bumping this up would break older
    machines that otherwise support Apple Silicon.

    Note: XML 1.0 Section 2.5 forbids the double-hyphen sequence inside
    comments, which is why flags like the version CLI flag are spelled
    without the literal prefix in this note.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
    <string>en</string>
    <key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
    <string>splitlane</string>
    <key>CFBundleIconFile</key>
    <string>Splitlane</string>
    <!--
        The identifier macOS keys everything to: window state, notification
        settings, and the TCC grants for screen recording and accessibility.
        Changing it makes this a different application to the OS, and every
        grant a person made is asked for again.

        `ivkan` is the handle the update feed names (`update/checker.rs`),
        and the last part is the product name. scripts/bundle-macos.sh signs
        with this same identifier, so the two cannot drift.
    -->
    <key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
    <string>io.github.ivkan.splitlane</string>
    <key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
    <string>6.0</string>
    <key>CFBundleName</key>
    <string>Splitlane</string>
    <key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
    <string>APPL</string>
    <key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
    <string>@VERSION@</string>
    <key>CFBundleSupportedPlatforms</key>
    <array>
        <string>MacOSX</string>
    </array>
    <key>CFBundleVersion</key>
    <string>@VERSION@</string>
    <key>LSApplicationCategoryType</key>
    <string>public.app-category.developer-tools</string>
    <key>LSMinimumSystemVersion</key>
    <string>13.0</string>
    <key>NSHighResolutionCapable</key>
    <true/>
    <!--
        macOS gates the Desktop, Documents and Downloads folders behind TCC,
        and it attributes what a child process reads to the application that
        spawned it. Every pane here is a PTY, so a shell command or a coding
        agent reaching into one of those folders makes the OS ask on this
        application's behalf. That is correct: the app itself never enumerates
        them (its own reads are the project directory, the agent CLIs' session
        stores and its own config), and suppressing the question is neither
        possible nor wanted.

        What these keys change is that the dialog can say why. Without them
        macOS shows the bare request with no explanation, which reads as an
        application helping itself to a folder rather than as the command the
        person just ran. Keep them one sentence: the dialog truncates.
    -->
    <key>NSDesktopFolderUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Coding agents and shell commands running in Splitlane's panes read and write the files you point them at.</string>
    <key>NSDocumentsFolderUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Coding agents and shell commands running in Splitlane's panes read and write the files you point them at.</string>
    <key>NSDownloadsFolderUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Coding agents and shell commands running in Splitlane's panes read and write the files you point them at.</string>
    <key>NSRemovableVolumesUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Coding agents and shell commands running in Splitlane's panes read and write the files you point them at.</string>
    <key>NSNetworkVolumesUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Coding agents and shell commands running in Splitlane's panes read and write the files you point them at.</string>
    <!--
        The microphone is the same attribution and a much harsher failure. A
        folder key that is missing only costs the dialog its explanation; a
        missing NSMicrophoneUsageDescription means TCC cannot ask at all, so it
        kills the requesting process outright - SIGABRT from
        __TCC_CRASHING_DUE_TO_PRIVACY_VIOLATION__, before that process has
        written a line. Measured on macOS 26.6.2: `ffmpeg -f avfoundation`
        recording a call in a pane died in 0.7 s with an empty log and a crash
        report naming this bundle as the coalition. Nothing in the pane can
        diagnose that, because the process never ran.
    -->
    <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Recording and transcription commands you run in Splitlane's panes capture audio through this application.</string>
    <!--
        Photos and Music are the folder attribution reached a different way:
        not by a command pointed at them, but by one searching a folder they
        sit under. An agent started in the home folder indexes its working
        directory, walks into the Photos library and ~/Music, and the OS asks
        on this application's behalf, once for each. Reported from a first
        launch on a second machine (10 September), when the app still opened
        the home folder as a project on its own; that is fixed, and these stay
        for the agent a person starts there on purpose. Not measured: whether
        the file-access path shows these two strings the way it shows the
        folder keys above.
    -->
    <key>NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Coding agents and shell commands running in Splitlane's panes reach your Photos library when they search a folder that contains it.</string>
    <key>NSAppleMusicUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Coding agents and shell commands running in Splitlane's panes reach your Music library when they search a folder that contains it.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
